event: response.output_text.done
data: {"type":"response.output_text.done","content_index":0,"item_id":"msg_07f22660fe476212006a74db8c9aec8196a813b8bda4f1dd65","logprobs":[],"output_index":4,"sequence_number":333,"text":"The latest Perseverance news is that NASA/JPL reported on Aug. 5, 2026, that the rover captured Earth disappearing behind Mars’ moon Phobos — the first time such an “Earth occultation” has been observed from the surface of another planet. Perseverance took the image sequence with its Mastcam-Z camera on July 2, 2026, during sol 1,907 of the mission, from the rim of Jezero Crater【5:0†source】.\n\nA few key details:\n\n- Earth appears as a tiny bright dot in the Martian sky, then slips behind the crescent of Phobos and reappears.\n- Phobos was much closer to Perseverance — about 4,850 miles / 7,800 km away — while Earth was about 195 million miles / 314 million km away at the time【5:0†source】.\n- NASA described it as a unique “Earth self-portrait” from another planet, with Phobos “photobombing” the view【5:0†source】.\n\nOther recent Perseverance updates include a July 15, 2026 report that the rover found evidence that a 245-foot / 75-meter stack of ancient rock at Jezero Crater’s rim — called the “Broom Point member” — was likely built by repeated asteroid impacts, offering clues about very early Mars, possibly more than 3.9 billion years ago【5:3†source】. NASA’s image pages also show the rover is still returning new raw images, including “Image of the Week” selections for late July–early August 2026【5:1†source】."}

